## The specification and the repository's lineage

- F-173 **The specification's type-inference chapter states the rule that the compiled checker builds: the declaration is chosen first, then its static arguments are inferred.** It states P-46, P-48 and P-49. A type parameter that is the whole declared type of one or more parameters, as `T` is of `x: T`, takes the narrowest of those arguments' types and the types to which they can be coerced; where none is narrowest, it takes its bound. A type parameter that nothing fixes takes its bound, never `BottomType`. The chapter's callouts give two departures of the compiled path: its prelude lacks the conversion between `ZZ32` and `NN32` (row 442), and it bounds a type parameter written without an `extends` clause by `Object`, not `Any` (row 412, P-42). They also give walk's departures, such as the `BottomType` result of a function expression that declares no return type (row 587) (`Specification/basic/inference.tex:292-295` and `:356-425`). Plain tests gate the first rule on both paths: `compiler_tests/InferLone*` and `tests/InferLone*Walk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/REPORT.md` (the bound rule); `Specification/basic/inference.tex:47-124` (the chapter's rule).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f16/proposals.md, "F-173"); "departs only at row 442" widened to the chapter's two compiled departures, row 412 added from inference.tex:37-40 and :356-363 and P-42; "lone type parameter" said in the chapter's words (inference.tex:77-78); the bare list of walk's rows (587, 589, 591, 592, 616; the callouts also name 20, 307, 510, 716, 717, 760, 761 and 762) replaced by one example and the callouts' lines; the route moved to rung-instance-bound, since rung-spec-inference/decision-record.md section 3.1 states the rule that it superseded
- F-174 **The specification states the type group's 2011 overloading model: declarations of one name may differ in their static parameters, and a declaration with static parameters is read over its instances.** It states P-45 and P-42. The section "Declarations with Static Parameters" states the OOPSLA 2011 paper's three rules: No Duplicates, Return Type and Meet. It gives one more restriction as the compiled path's, not the language's: where one generic declaration is more specific than another with as many static parameters, the two must declare them of the same kinds, position by position (row 398). A functional whose single parameter's type is a type parameter with the bound `Any` written, as `T extends Any`, cannot be overloaded; a type parameter with no `extends` clause does not fall under this rule, and walk applies it (row 534, fixed). Neither path runs every set that the rules allow. Appendix I's entry "Overloading with static parameters" names the sets that each path fails: row 542 (the checker, a more specific declaration over an object type), row 537 (the code generator, two declarations whose bounds differ), row 539 (walk, the Meet Rule's search), and rows 779 and 780 (walk, two generic functional methods one inside the other). Detail: `Specification/advanced/overloading.tex:134-150` and `:531-674`; `compile-ladder/rung-spec-overloading/decision-record.md` sections 3 and 4.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f16/proposals.md, "F-174"); Appendix I's rows brought to its current text (changes.tex:2856-2879): row 159, fixed at 6d187d069 and no longer named there, dropped, rows 779 and 780 added, each row given its subject; the naked-Any rule and the positional restriction said in words (overloading.tex:134-150, :644-652)
- F-175 **The specification reads a `comprises` clause at the level of values in every passage.** It builds P-60. Appendix I's entry "Traits with comprises clauses read at the level of values" records the change. Appendix I names the compiled checker's departures from it: the checker checks a `comprises` clause at the closed trait's immediate subtypes only (row 487); it crashes on a method call on a result typed by the intersection of its candidates' return types (row 540); in a family with static parameters, it types such a call by the head of a sort (row 543) and refuses the Meet Rule's case for closed traits (row 546); and its verdict on an overload set can depend on the components compiled before it in the same JVM (row 547). Detail: `Specification/appendices/changes.tex:3185-3240`; `compile-ladder/rung-spec-overloading/decision-record.md` sections 5 to 9.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f16/proposals.md, "F-175"); split: the dispatched instance (P-48; rows 538 and 496) is now F-?1, and this entry keeps the comprises half, which P-60's "Built" line names; rows 487, 540, 543, 546 and 547 given their subjects from their ledger cells
- F-?1 **The specification gives the run-time instance of a dispatched generic declaration by the POPL 2019 paper's rule.** It builds P-48. Where the value does not fix a type parameter, the rule gives the parameter its bound under the call's static return type. The dispatch paragraph's callout says that both paths take the value's type there instead (row 538, the expected failure `compiler_tests/XXXGenericInstanceUnfixed`). It also says that the compiled path does not yet run its second example, a generic declaration beside a plain one, reached through an argument of static type `Any` (row 496). F-173 gives the inference chapter. Detail: `Specification/basic/overloading.tex:312-349`; `compile-ladder/rung-spec-overloading/decision-record.md` sections 5 to 9.
  by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-175 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f16/proposals.md, "F-?1"); the rule's bound and the second example said from rows 538 and 496
- F-176 **The specification allows arithmetic in a size, and two size arguments are identical when they are written alike once each operation on numerals is replaced by its value.** It states P-55. By the grammar, a size is a numeral, a name, a sum, a difference, a product, a power to a numeral or a name, or a bracketed size. Two types whose size arguments are so identical are identical. A static parameter is not known to differ from a numeral. F-146 gives the compiled checker, and F-222 walk's arithmetic. When walk infers a size parameter from a call's argument type, it matches a numeral and binds a name, and solves no size written as an operation: read in `IntNat.unifyStaticArg` (`interpreter/evaluator/types/IntNat.java:126-147`), and observed in row 767. Source: `Specification/basic/types-vals-vars.tex:49-62`; `Specification/appendices/grammars/concrete-syntax.tex:533-547` (`IntExpr`).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f16/proposals.md, "F-176"); the title worded as identity of size arguments, not of size expressions as types (types-vals-vars.tex:51-58), with "folded" said in words; the forms cited to the grammar; the walk sentence scoped to inference from a call's argument type and its evidence named, the code read and row 767
- F-223 **The specification's No Duplicates Rule is the papers' non-strict rule, and the compiled checker applies it, to functional methods wherever their `self` parameters stand, abstract ones included, with two exceptions.** By the rule, two declarations of one name may not each be applicable to every call to which the other is, as in the type group's papers of 2011 and 2019 (P-26). The checker refuses such a pair, two functional methods at different `self` positions and an abstract functional method beside a concrete one among them: "There are multiple declarations of f with the same parameter type", naming the domain of the declaration written first. It checks no symbolic operator (row 584), so such a pair of operators passes it and stops the code generator (row 726). It accepts `g[\T\](a: T)` beside `g(a: Any)` and runs the generic one, though it refuses such a pair at a bound below `Any` (row 759). Walk's refusal of such pairs is in F-252, and its message in F-257. Gated by `compiler_tests/XXXNoDuplicatesRule*`: the checker's refusals, and its departure at an operator (`XXXNoDuplicatesRuleSelfPositionsOperator`). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-popl-examples/REPORT.md` section 4, its `SKEPTIC.md` section 2, `compile-ladder/rung-duplicates-coverage/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 8, and `compile-ladder/rung-checker-abstract-callsite/REPORT.md` section 1.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f16/proposals.md, "F-223"); row 759 added as the checker's second exception, which F-252 already states; walk's half left to F-252 and F-257, which own it; the nine tests given as a pattern; moved here: walk's examples dup[\T\](x: Box[\T\]) beside dup[\U\](x: Box[\U\]) and the generic beside the declaration at its bounds, and walk's gates tests/NoDuplicatesRuleWalk, GenericBesidePlainSameDomain and GenericBesideGenericSameDomain, which F-252 and F-257 name

## The territory map

- F-180 **`import java` is wired on walk through `ForeignComponentWrapper` and `ClosureMaker`, but five gaps stop it and no test uses it.** In a probe, six small Java patches closed the five gaps, and a two-declaration `import java` component then printed on walk what the compiled path prints. P-58 binds the natives another way: a `builtinPrimitive("…")` body whose string names a static Java helper. Both halves of that way ran as probes: walk's loader of such a helper, and the compiled path's rewrite of such a body into a call, at the parse. Two findings bear on any binding that both paths share. A helper raises a Fortress exception by naming a class of the compiled prelude, such as `fortress.CompilerBuiltin$IntegerOverflow`; walk has no such class, so without the probe's fifth patch it ends the run with a `FortressImplementationError`. A helper whose parameters or results are compiled-path classes, such as `FZZ32Vector` or `CompilerBuiltin$IntLiteral`, cannot take walk's values; the helpers have 50 such parameters or results. Detail: `perf-probes/prelude/natives-shape.md` section 1 (walk's half: gap 5, and "Limits, by reading"); `perf-probes/prelude/natives-binding-probe.md` sections 1 and 2 (the compiled half).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f16/proposals.md, "F-180"); the two halves named; "compiled-world types" said with two examples and the count, 6 + 13 + 17 + 14 (natives-shape.md, "Limits, by reading"); natives-binding-probe.md sections 1 and 2 restored to the route, since natives-shape.md says that the compiled half is not measured
- F-181 **The code generator refuses every function, object and trait declaration that carries a `where` clause in its own header, and every function with a contract (row 701).** A `where` clause on an `extends` entry, and a trait's or object's contract, do not reach this refusal; F-255 says how each path reads them (rows 774 and 781). The `fortress-repo` skill's `references/compiler.md`, "What the compiled path cannot compile yet", gives this refusal and the constructs that the code generator has no case for, a local function among them (row 304). An array comprehension has no case either. In a probe on a scratch copy of `CodeGen.java` that let the guard through, the library's `trait NatParam comprises { N[\n\] } where [\ nat n \]`, whose `where` clause binds a size and states no constraint, compiled and ran; opening its value at a call failed verification (row 307). Detail: `coordinator/map/spec-to-implementation.md` section 3; `perf-probes/where-clauses/REPORT.md` A.7 (the probe).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f16/proposals.md, "F-181"); rows 774 and 781 left to F-255, which owns them; the NatParam result marked as a measurement on a scratch copy (where-clauses/REPORT.md A.7), and NatParam named (NatReflect.fsi:23)
- F-182 **Both paths refuse a `type` alias, a `test` declaration of the form `test Id[gens] = Expr`, and a `property` declaration, in the index builder that both paths run (`scala_src/typechecker/IndexBuilder.scala:187-189`).** Each stops its component with `** bug! Not yet implemented:` and the declaration's kind, such as `TestDecl` (rows 18, 121 and 122). A function with the `test` modifier is another form, and it runs under `fortress test` (row 120).
